# LEGACY-RME-PDF-1D — VOID and clinical read/print

1. **VOID retracts, it never erases.** The row, its pages and its files on the
   private disk all survive. Only `status`, `voided_by`, `voided_at` and
   `void_reason` are written; patient, date, file, checksums and pages are never
   touched, so a "correction" can never be smuggled in as a void.

2. **VOID is terminal.** `PUBLISHED → VOID` and nothing out of VOID. There is no
   un-void, no republish and no delete route, because a published archive is
   clinical evidence.

3. **Correction is a fresh import.** Retract the wrong archive, then import the
   right one. Never edit a published record in place.

4. **A reason is mandatory** (min 10 characters), enforced in the FormRequest
   *and* again in the service, since a FormRequest only guards the HTTP door.
   The reason is stored on the record. It is deliberately **not** written into
   the audit payload — it is operator free text that may name a patient, and the
   audit allow-list is structure-only (`void_reason_length` records that a real
   explanation was given).

5. **Atomic and idempotent.** One transaction opening with a row lock that
   re-reads the status. A second void is a no-op that preserves the FIRST reason
   and actor, and writes no second audit row.

6. **No downstream side effects.** Voiding writes to one table. It creates no
   visit, medical record, invoice, payment, consent, odontogram, lab
   candidate/order or SATUSEHAT candidate, and never touches a visit status.

7. **A VOIDed archive stops serving bytes.** Source, pages, print and export all
   refuse it. The row itself stays readable so the metadata and the reason
   remain auditable — retracted, not erased.

8. **Reading is a different permission from operating.**
   `view_legacy_rme_imports` is the intake operator; `view_legacy_rme_archive`
   is the clinical reader (doctor). Either may READ; neither implies the other,
   and neither implies review/publish/void.

9. **The clinical read permission is never governance tier.** It must stay out
   of `LegacyRmeWorkspaceScope::GOVERNANCE_PERMISSIONS`, or a doctor would widen
   from their own branch to every RME branch. Rows with no branch provenance
   stay governance-only.

10. **Out of scope is 404, never 403.** A reader must not be able to probe which
    archive ids exist in a branch they cannot see.

11. **Enforce state in the controller, not only the policy.** The single global
    `Gate::before` grants Super Admin every ability, and Super Admin is exactly
    who operates this capability, so a policy-only rule is no rule at all for
    the actor that matters. The policy keeps the same check as defence in depth.

12. **Print and export are never a weaker door.** Both sit behind auth, the
    permission, the policy and the feature flag; both refuse a VOIDed record.
    Print REFERENCES pages through the policy-gated page route (the browser
    re-requests each with the caller's own session); export embeds them through
    the storage abstraction. No absolute path, no public URL, no signed direct
    link, no storage symlink.

13. **Output is visibly a legacy archive.** Print and export both carry
    "ARSIP RME LAMA — DOKUMEN HISTORIS (HANYA BACA)" and state that it is not a
    visit, bill, payment or lab order. It must never read as native RME output.

14. **Never render an identity field.** KTP/NIK never appears in the viewer,
    print or export, and the download filename is generic — no patient name and
    no medical-record number.

15. **dompdf output stays table-based** (no flexbox/grid, no external asset) and
    the export is bounded; past the cap it says so and points at the source PDF.
    Rendering embedded pages requires the GD extension.

16. **The feature flag still governs everything.** With
    `rme.legacy_pdf_archive` off, every 1D route answers 404 server-side.